summary:
This contents page (printed xii in the header) covers the language-modeling core of the book.

**14.5, Sequence models (printed 405, PDF p.429):** training a recurrent model, understanding word embeddings, using a word embedding, pretraining a word embedding, and using that pretrained embedding for classification.

**Chapter 15, Language models and the Transformer (printed 421, PDF p.445):**
- 15.1 the language model — training and generating with a Shakespeare language model;
- 15.2 sequence-to-sequence learning — English-to-Spanish translation, first with RNNs;
- 15.3 the Transformer architecture — dot-product attention, encoder block, decoder block, sequence-to-sequence with a Transformer, and embedding positional information;
- 15.4 classification with a pretrained Transformer — pretraining an encoder, loading a pretrained one, preprocessing IMDb reviews and fine-tuning;
- 15.5 what makes the Transformer effective?

**Chapter 16, Text generation (printed 466, PDF p.490):**
- 16.1 a brief history of sequence generation;
- 16.2 training a mini-GPT — building and pretraining the model, generative decoding and sampling strategies;
- 16.3 using a pretrained LLM — text generation with Gemma, instruction fine-tuning and Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA);
- 16.4 going further with LLMs — RLHF, multimodal LLMs, retrieval augmented generation (RAG) and "reasoning" models;
- 16.5 where LLMs are heading next.

The progression mirrors the vision chapters: build small models yourself to understand the mechanism, then use large pretrained ones.
